---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import LegalIdentity from '../../components/LegalIdentity.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, AUTHOR_DESC, CONTACT_EMAIL, SITE_NAMES } from '../../data/site-config';
import { srcLink } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { profilePageSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('about', lang);
const title = 'Who publishes BR Feriados: Radif Partners and its sources';
const description = 'Who publishes this Brazilian public holiday calculator: Radif Partners, independent and selling no trips. Where the national and state holidays come from.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[profilePageSchema({ path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'About', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'About' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAMES[lang]}</h1>
    <p>{AUTHOR_DESC.en}</p>
    <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
    <p>In Brazil the question "is tomorrow a holiday?" has no single answer. Nine national dates apply everywhere, Good Friday almost everywhere, and then each state and each city adds its own: Rio de Janeiro has Carnival Tuesday and Saint George's Day, Acre moves its state holidays to Fridays, Santa Catarina celebrates its state day on a Sunday. Most holiday lists copy one another, mix optional days off with real holidays and never say which law they read. This site lists each date with the law behind it and the day we last read it, and leaves out what we could not confirm in an official text.</p>
    <h2>Who publishes it</h2>
    <p>The publisher is {AUTHOR_NAME}, a company that builds tools from public rules and data. {AUTHOR_NAME} is not a public body or a law firm, has no link with the federal, state or municipal governments of Brazil and uses no coats of arms or official logos. The site sells no trips, hotels or packages and carries no affiliate links.</p>
    <LegalIdentity lang={lang} />
    <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <p>National holidays come from <Fragment set:html={s('lei-662', 'Law 662/1949')} />, <Fragment set:html={s('lei-6802', 'Law 6,802/1980')} /> (12 October) and <Fragment set:html={s('lei-14759', 'Law 14,759/2023')} /> (20 November); Good Friday from <Fragment set:html={s('lei-9093', 'Law 9,093/1995')} />. Federal optional days off come from the Ministry of Management's yearly ordinance, for 2026 the <Fragment set:html={s('portaria-mgi-2026', 'MGI ordinance 11,460/2025')} />. State holidays come from each state's laws, constitution or decree, such as Acre's <Fragment set:html={s('ac-lei-2126', 'Law 2,126/2009')} /> on moving holidays to Friday. Capital-city holidays are taken one by one from the municipal law; a capital whose law we could not read is not listed. Last full reading: {displayDate(K.LIDO_EM, 'en-GB')}. How dates are computed is on the <a href={route('method', lang)}>method</a> page; how we correct mistakes is in the <a href={route('editorial', lang)}>editorial policy</a>.</p>
    <h2>Your data</h2>
    <p>Every calculator runs in your browser. The state, city and days off you enter are neither sent nor stored. There is no account and no form. Details are in the <a href={route('privacy', lang)}>privacy notice</a>.</p>
    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>It does not tell you whether your employer must give you a day off: bridge days, Carnival and optional days off depend on your contract, collective agreement or, for civil servants, the government's decree. Bank and court calendars have their own rules. School holidays are not covered.</p>
    <h2>Write to us</h2>
    <p><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a> if a holiday, a date or a law looks wrong.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
